First Author: Singh
Title: Predicting post-lung transplant survival in systemic sclerosis using CT-derived features from preoperative chest CT scans
Source number: 49  ￼

Very short summary: This retrospective single-center study investigated whether CT-derived body-composition and cardiopulmonary features from preoperative chest CT improve survival prediction after lung transplantation in systemic sclerosis. Five key findings were: (1) 102 systemic sclerosis recipients were included, with mean age 50 years and 61% female; (2) 49.0% died during follow-up, with Kaplan–Meier survival rates of 76.3% at 1 year, 64.3% at 3 years, and 52.2% at 5 years; (3) several CT-derived features were significantly associated with survival, including muscle mass ratio, bone density, artery–vein volume ratio, muscle volume, and heart volume ratio; (4) models using only CT-derived features outperformed state-of-the-art clinical models; and (5) adding CT-derived features to demographic and clinical models improved prediction, reaching maximum AUCs of 0.77 for 1-year, 0.85 for 3-year, and 0.90 for 5-year survival. Figures 1–2 and Tables 1–3 were directly used for this extraction.  ￼

