Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: This was a single-center retrospective cohort of only 102 systemic sclerosis recipients, which limits generalizability despite being substantial for this rare population.  ￼
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Predictors were clinically relevant demographic and preoperative clinical factors plus objectively derived CT body-composition and cardiopulmonary features generated with previously validated algorithms.  ￼
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Survival was clearly defined with right censoring at the study cutoff, and Kaplan–Meier plus Cox proportional-hazards methods were used appropriately for time-to-event analysis.  ￼
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Strengths include multivariable Cox modeling, time-dependent ROC-AUC evaluation, c-index and Brier-score reporting, and 5-fold cross-validation. Uncertainty remains because the study is single-center and the snippets do not show external validation.  ￼
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The article directly addresses AI/CT-enhanced prediction of post-lung-transplant survival and is highly relevant, although focused on the specific systemic sclerosis subgroup.  ￼

